efinition</string><string id="acc_definition">definition</string><string id="acc_resources">resources</string><string id="acc_search_input">search</string><string id="acc_pause">pause</string><string id="acc_play">play</string><string id="acc_replay">replay</string><string id="acc_submit">submit</string><string id="acc_next">next</string><string id="acc_previous">previous</string><string id="acc_volume">volume</string></string_table></string_tables><sounds enabled="false" /><nav_data><outline><links><slidelink slideid="_player.6VoLvSI2wO2" displaytext="Data Center Power Efficiencies" expand="true"><links><slidelink slideid="_player.6VoLvSI2wO2.5ZCNh4bn1iW" displaytext="Data Center Power Efficiencies" expand="true" /><slidelink slideid="_player.6VoLvSI2wO2.6Kofwd3zvUR" displaytext="PUE vs. DCiE" expand="true" /><slidelink slideid="_player.6VoLvSI2wO2.6ddfEHq2BU1" displaytext="PUE vs. DCiE" expand="true" /><slidelink slideid="_player.6VoLvSI2wO2.6poX2ZRX57g" displaytext="Example" expand="true" /><slidelink slideid="_player.6VoLvSI2wO2.6B3AwN5T5d1" displaytext="PUE and DCiE" expand="true" /><slidelink slideid="_player.6VoLvSI2wO2.6I3Axa0mkSH" displaytext="How Efficient?" expand="true" /><slidelink slideid="_player.6VoLvSI2wO2.6e9U07AIYNy" displaytext="How do they get better?" expand="true" /><slidelink slideid="_player.6VoLvSI2wO2.5w4NtfwiYKP" displaytext="CC-BY 3.0" expand="true" /></links></slidelink></links></outline><search><slidetext slideid="6VoLvSI2wO2.5ZCNh4bn1iW" slidebank="false">data center power efficiencies determining pue and dcie </slidetext><slidetext slideid="6VoLvSI2wO2.6Kofwd3zvUR" slidebank="false">pue vs. dcie power usage efficiency (pue) expressed as unitless measure total energy usage / it energy usage data center infrastructure efficiency (dcie) expressed as a percentage it energy usage / total energy usage two variables, two calculations, two measures of efficiency </slidetext><slidetext slideid="6VoLvSI2wO2.6ddfEHq2BU1" slidebank="false">pue vs. dcie this chart shows standard values for data center power efficiencies. </slidetext><slidetext slideid="6VoLvSI2wO2.6poX2ZRX57g" slidebank="false">example: organization x organization x reports the following: it energy load: 6.000 kw (kilowatts) total energy load: 9.500 kw what is  pue? dcie? how power-efficient is organization x?  </slidetext><slidetext slideid="6VoLvSI2wO2.6B3AwN5T5d1" slidebank="false">organization x: doing the math total energy usage / it energy usage = pue therefore: pue = 9.500 kw / 6.000 kw pue = 1.583 (approx) it energy usage = dcie therefore: dcie = 6.000 kw / 9.500 kw dcie = 0.632 or 63.2%  </slidetext><slidetext slideid="6VoLvSI2wO2.6I3Axa0mkSH" slidebank="false">they could do better. according to our chart organization x is efficient but... how efficient is organization x? </slidetext><slidetext slideid="6VoLvSI2wO2.6e9U07AIYNy" slidebank="false">how do they get better? to become “very efficient,” organization x would have to reduce pue to 1.2 or less increase dcie to 83% or more given a total load of 9.500 kw, organization x would have to increase it energy load to 8.000 kw (approx) given fixed total energy load of 9.500 kw 1.2 = 9.500 kw / x 9.500 kw / 8.000 kw = 1.19 pue 8.000 kw / 9.500 = 84 % dcie decrease total energy load if it energy load is fixed at 6.000 kw 1.2 = x / 6.000 kw total load would have to be 7.200 kw  </slidetext><slidetext slideid="6VoLvSI2wO2.5w4NtfwiYKP" slidebank="false">adapted from content provided by karl parr, associate professor at wake community college data center power efficiencies: determining pue and dcie by karl parr is license cc-by 3.0 </slidetext></search></nav_data><resource_data description="&lt;p align='left'&gt;&lt;font face='Articulate' size='11' color='#FFFFFF'&gt;Here are some useful links and documents:&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;" /><transcript_data /><glossary_data /></bwFrame>